Here is a complete setup for working with assembly (nasm, gcc, make) and gdb inside a Docker container while keeping your local files synced.
Because gdb needs special permissions to trace processes, you must run the container with the --privileged flag or specific security capabilities.
1. The Dockerfile
Create a file named Dockerfile in your project directory:
dockerfile
FROM ubuntu:24.04

# Prevent interactive prompts during installation
ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

# Install assembly tools, compilers, debugger, and essentials
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y \
    build-essential \
    nasm \
    nano \
    gdb \
    make \
    gcc \
    binutils \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Set up the working directory inside the container
WORKDIR /workspace

# Keep the container alive if no command is given
CMD ["/bin/bash"]

Use code with caution.

2. How to Build and Run (With Volumes)
Run these commands in your terminal from the same folder where your Dockerfile and local assembly source files are located.
Step 1: Build the image
bash
docker build -t asm-env .

Use code with caution.
Step 2: Run the container with a volume and debugger privileges
macOS / Linux (Bash/Zsh):
bash
docker run -it --privileged -v "$(pwd)":/workspace asm-env
Use code with caution.
Windows (PowerShell):
bash
docker run -it --privileged -v "${PWD}:/workspace" asm-env
Use code with caution.
Windows (Command Prompt):
bash
docker run -it --privileged -v "%cd%":/workspace asm-env
Use code with caution.
Why is --privileged required?
By default, Docker restricts system calls like ptrace, which gdb relies on to control 
and inspect your code. The --privileged flag grants the container the access it needs 
to debug successfully.

3. Quick Workflow Example
Once you are inside the container's interactive shell, you can compile and debug immediately. 
Any files you create or modify will save directly to your local host machine.
Create an assembly file (on your local machine or inside the container):

nasm
; hello.asm
section .data
    msg db "Hello, Assembly!", 10
    len equ $ - msg

section .text
    global _start

_start:
    mov eax, 4      ; sys_write
    mov ebx, 1      ; stdout
    mov ecx, msg
    mov edx, len
    int 0x80        ; kernel call

    mov eax, 1      ; sys_exit
    mov ebx, 0      ; return 0
    int 0x80


Assemble and link with debug symbols (-g):
bash
nasm -f elf32 -g hello.asm -o hello.o
ld -m elf_i386 hello.o -o hello
Use code with caution.
Debug with GDB:
bash
gdb ./hello
Use code with caution.
